WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.460
All right, you guys ready?

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.560
Agency fees, which are
the fees non-members are forced to pay

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.920
unions for representation in some states –
and the Abood precedent that allows them –

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:17.160
could both be scrapped when the Supreme Court hears Janus versus AFSCME. So why

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.150
the fight over agency fees? Unions call
these fees "fair share fees" because

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:25.199
everybody covered by a union-negotiated
contract has to pay for representation.

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:28.949
The fees are typically two-thirds of
full membership dues, and they aren't

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:32.340
supposed to support unions' political
activities, balancing freedom of

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.510
expression with the need to prevent free
riders. The petitioner, Janus, argues that

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.680
agency fees infringe on freedoms of
speech and association, claiming it's

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.489
impossible to separate unions' political
and non-political functions when union

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:48.030
bargaining with the government is
inherently political. For Janus, it's not

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.989
about the money – it's about compelled
speech. "Stare decisis" is a legal

00:00:51.989 --> 00:00:55.559
principle that makes overturning
precedents rare, but many court watchers,

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:59.850
including unions, expect the conservative
justices will overturn Abood. Whether

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:03.690
the court decides agency fees are an
appropriate balance or compelled speech

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:07.220
will have a major influence on the
future of America's unions.

